    "created_at": "2022-09-26T00:04:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-09-26T00:04:46Z",
    "body": "Sorry for beating a dead horse here, but this is just a sad state of affairs for a project that strives to be an international standard.\r\nNo one's \"_bizarre and private poetics_\" (to quote @anthonygood) should be codified in a standard without a good reason, and one single person's opinion that the correct past tense of a particular English verb \"_sounded dumb_\" (quote @Hixie) hardly qualifies as a good reason.\r\nAs for the reasoning of keeping things \"_more approachable for non-native speakers_\" (quote @annevk), I can only say that, as a non-native speaker, I find this confusing as hell, and it really has the opposite effect. Probably in 6th grade I was ~teached~ taught that the past tense of _seek_ is _sought_, and yet, in the HTML standard, the past tense is \"_seeked_\"? Is there really anyone out there who finds this helpful?\r\nWhat really *hasn't* been answered is \"_what process or discussion preceded its inclusion_\" (@anthonygood), and what has been done to prevent made-up words from someone's personal dictionary from appearing in future versions of the standard. Or does the community indeed agree that \"Hixie English\" is the language of WHATWG standards? (I sure hope not, otherwise, I sadly have to agree with with @anthonygood's \"_bunch of deviants_\" comment...)\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
